+
+Framebuffer fetch without native ordered attachment access or pixel interlock can be
+compiled under `FramebufferFetchPolicy::AcceptUnordered`. The runtime orders a bounded
+subset exactly with input attachments, one triangle per Vulkan draw and subpass memory
+barriers: single-sample, single-layer direct triangle lists, one instance, at most 4096
+triangles per draw, no vertex or fragment memory writes, primitive ID input, tessellation,
+mesh stage, layered output or vertex amplification. Unsupported shapes still require
+native ordered attachment access. The compiler's unordered-fetch precision note is kept;
+the runtime supplies ordering for the admitted draws. `RequireOrdered` remains the
+default compile policy. Native ordered and interlocked paths keep their existing behavior.
+
+Under `PaddedReadsPolicy::Shadow`, a texture over an emulated no-copy buffer uses a
+separate image even for packed rows. Native image layout transitions can write bytes
+when the Metal command only reads the texture; those representation writes stay in
+the separate image instead of becoming a host-buffer output. The aliasing policies
+retain their existing fast paths. This adds GPU copies where such a texture is used.
+
+Host synchronization uses logical buffer accesses independently of automatic hazard
+tracking. A known input is not copied back or invalidated merely because its hazards
+are untracked; actual writes and resources with unknown access still synchronize to
+the host. This does not introduce queue dependencies for untracked resources.
+
+A shadow image's initialization and input copies do not make its backing program
+buffer a host output. After resolving memory owners, completion checks the logical
+buffer's own accesses; a real shadow copy back still records that buffer as written.
+
+An emulated no-copy buffer keeps one CPU-cached snapshot of its last transferred
+host bytes, costing at most one extra buffer-sized allocation until that buffer is
+released. Allocation failure reports host out-of-memory. Upload compares CPU bytes
+with that snapshot, never with a write-combining GPU mapping, and transfers only
+changed byte runs; unchanged CPU input does not replace newer GPU values. Completion
+stages each output chunk once in cached CPU memory before comparison and copyback.
+Native imported host memory does not use this snapshot.
